WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:18.580
This is SUI, a tattoo stencil studio that runs in your browser. In the next few minutes you will see the whole thing. A client photo turned into a stencil, lettering in any Indian language, and how to print it at the exact size you are going to tattoo.

2
00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:37.160
Sign in with Google. Every new account gets ten free stencils. No card, no trial period, nothing to cancel. After that, credits are nine rupees each, and one credit draws one stencil.

3
00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:52.220
Start with your client photo. Drag it in, paste from the clipboard, or browse for it. A clear, well lit face works best. The sharper the photo, the cleaner the linework you get back.

4
00:00:53.020 --> 00:01:21.560
Now pick how you want it drawn. Studio stencil gives clean linework with stippled skin. Fine line is thin and minimal, with plenty of open skin. Bold traditional has heavy outlines and chunky dot shading. Dotwork builds every tone from dots alone. And realism map gives you every contour plus the shadow boundaries, for black and grey work.

5
00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:33.220
Press Ink it. It takes about thirty to forty seconds. Every run is a fresh drawing, so if the likeness is not right, simply run it again.

6
00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:43.200
Here is the stencil. Use the tabs above it to compare against the original photo, so you can check the likeness before you commit to it.

7
00:01:44.000 --> 00:02:03.420
Now finish it for your machine. Choose the ink colour. Turn on hard black and white to threshold it to solid ink for a thermal stencil printer. Mirror it so the design reads correctly once it is on the skin. And drop the background out if you want a transparent file.

8
00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:21.440
Download opens the size dialog, and this is the part most people get wrong. Set the finished size in millimetres, centimetres or inches. The proportions stay locked, so change one and the other follows.

9
00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:43.420
Fit one copy on the sheet, or fill an A4 with as many as it holds. Print with scaling set to one hundred percent, never fit to page. Then measure the fifty millimetre check bar on the sheet. If it measures fifty, your printer is honest and the stencil is true size.

10
00:02:44.220 --> 00:03:00.640
Switch to Lettering for script work. You do not need an Indian language keyboard. Type the name the way you say it. Amma becomes the Telugu word as you type. If a spelling looks wrong, tap one of the alternatives underneath.

11
00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:22.100
Choose a mood. Romantic, devotional, gothic, minimal, eighteen of them in all. Then weave a symbol into the letters themselves. A heart, a lotus, a mother and child. It shares a stroke with the letter, rather than sitting beside it.

12
00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:43.760
One important rule. The clean type version is typeset in a real font, so its spelling is correct by construction. The inked version is the drawing made from it. Compare the two, and have a native reader confirm it before any needle touches skin.

13
00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:58.340
When you run out, top up from the wallet. Ten credits for ninety rupees, up to a hundred credits for nine hundred. Pay by UPI, card, net banking or wallet.

14
00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:07.400
That is SUI. Go to sui dot ink, sign in, and your first ten stencils are on the house.
